│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ bounded_adaptive_kalman                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The current approach uses a two-state level/slope Kalman model with: initial measurement  │
│                          │ variance estimated from differenced MAD, covariance slope initialization of `0.1 *        │
│                          │ measurement_var`, innovation clipping at `3.5` standard deviations, base acceleration     │
│                          │ variance `0.0025 * measurement_var`, innovation adaptation beginning at `1.5` normalized  │
│                          │ innovation, measurement-noise learning rate `0.03`, and a two-sample display-level        │
│                          │ reversal gate.                                                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The new configuration retains the low-latency level/slope state model but replaces the    │
│                          │ thresholded process schedule and reversal hold with a bounded continuous adaptive         │
│                          │ process-noise schedule. The acceleration variance is now `clip(0.002 * R + 0.01 *         │
│                          │ innovation^2, 0.001 * R, 0.25 * R)`, which reacts quickly to sustained regime changes     │
│                          │ while remaining bounded during spikes. Measurement noise is updated more conservatively   │
│                          │ (`0.02`) using clipped residual energy. A continuous slope-aware output deadband replaces │
│                          │ the one-sample reversal hold, reducing false directional changes without introducing a    │
│                          │ discrete confirmation lag. The basic moving average is also vectorized while preserving   │
│                          │ its exact output interface.                                                               │
