│ patch_description        │ The current process noise is driven directly by the clipped innovation, so isolated       │
│                          │ measurement impulses can still raise the Kalman gain and produce many small               │
│                          │ output-direction changes. Replace the fixed clipping rule with an adaptive                │
│                          │ normalized-innovation gate and require evidence across consecutive samples before         │
│                          │ treating a large innovation as dynamic acceleration. A fast, bounded EWMA estimate of     │
│                          │ normalized innovation magnitude adapts the clipping threshold to changing noise regimes.  │
│                          │ In parallel, process-noise adaptation is based on persistent innovation energy rather     │
│                          │ than one sample. This should retain the current low lag on genuine transitions while      │
│                          │ materially reducing noise-induced slope changes and false reversals.                      │
