│ patch_name               │ bounded_kalman_hysteresis                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current approach uses a two-state level/slope Kalman filter with robust MAD-based     │
│                          │ measurement variance, innovation clipping at `3.0σ`, and bounded adaptive process         │
│                          │ variance (`baseline=0.002`, `innovation_weight=0.01`, `cap=0.25`). Its strong correlation │
│                          │ (0.94) and noise reduction (0.55) indicate that the core estimator should be retained,    │
│                          │ but its high slope-change and false-reversal counts indicate insufficient                 │
│                          │ output-direction stabilization.                                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This revision keeps the low-lag constant-velocity Kalman model while conservatively       │
│                          │ tuning adaptive process noise to `baseline=0.0015`, `innovation_weight=0.0075`, and       │
│                          │ `cap=0.30`. These settings reduce noise-driven slope acceleration while retaining bounded │
│                          │ response to sustained changes. A causal direction hysteresis layer is added only to the   │
│                          │ emitted output: insignificant counter-trend motions are held, and a reversal requires two │
│                          │ consecutive noise-significant observations. The internal Kalman state is never frozen,    │
│                          │ preserving responsiveness once a real reversal persists.                                  │
