│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ model_confirmed_turns                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ Strengthen the reversal gate with state-model directional confirmation. The existing      │
│                          │ persistence rule only examines successive output-candidate directions, so correlated      │
│                          │ noise can still create accepted reversals. A genuine turn is better supported when the    │
│                          │ innovation-driven slope update and the current filtered slope both agree with the         │
│                          │ proposed output direction.                                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This edit adds a short fast slope estimate from recent first differences, forms a         │
│                          │ consensus with the alpha-beta slope, and uses a normalized directional-strength test.     │
│                          │ Counter-direction moves receive immediate acceptance only when both model slopes agree    │
│                          │ and the movement is materially above noise. Otherwise, modest reversals require the       │
│                          │ existing persistence confirmation, with stronger model agreement allowing confirmation    │
│                          │ one sample sooner. This should reduce false reversals and slope changes while avoiding    │
│                          │ unnecessary lag at real turning points.                                                   │
│ num_applied              │ 3                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0346                                                                                   │
