│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ reliable_multiscale_tracker                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The prior tracker achieved good lag and correlation but produced excessive slope changes  │
│                          │ and false reversals because short-horizon derivative activity could dominate during noisy │
│                          │ or impulse-contaminated intervals. This version retains the causal alpha-beta             │
│                          │ architecture but adds reliability-weighted fast/slow slope blending.                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Key changes and parameters:                                                               │
│                          │ - `base_alpha=0.075`, `max_alpha=0.455`: lower quiet-state gain improves smoothness and   │
│                          │ noise reduction while retaining rapid adaptation on sustained innovations.                │
│                          │ - `base_beta=0.0022`, `max_beta=0.072`: reduces noisy slope-state oscillation and         │
│                          │ spurious directional reversals.                                                           │
│                          │ - Fast-model reliability is reduced when `fast_scale / slow_scale` is elevated or the     │
│                          │ newest endpoint residual is impulse-like.                                                 │
│                          │ - `fast_weight` is activity-dependent but multiplied by reliability, preventing           │
│                          │ contaminated short windows from forcing false turns.                                      │
│                          │ - Reversal gates use stronger bands (`reversal_band=0.46`, `immediate_band=1.28`) plus    │
│                          │ cumulative excursion and directional-model confirmation.                                  │
│                          │ - A reversal needs both sustained output displacement and reliable slope consensus;       │
│                          │ genuine strong turns can still pass quickly when fast and slow models agree.              │
│                          │ - Robust MAD/trimmed-RMS noise estimation and asymmetric volatility adaptation remain     │
│                          │ causal and computationally efficient.                                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The public inputs, outputs, validation behavior, output lengths, enhanced/basic modes,    │
│                          │ and non-finite sample handling are preserved.                                             │
